[00:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'button, one box, nothing else. So, here is my question for you. Why, after you open and close that little box a few hundred times, is your browser tab quietly sitting at over a gigabyte of'

[00:26] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: Chrome DevTools Memory tab displays "Detached HTMLDivElement", "Detached HTMLElement", "Detached Text", and "(closure) onClose" with counts of 512.
AUDIO: 'memory? You closed the box every single time. You watched it disappear, and yet something is still holding on. Your tab is holding your memory hostage and you probably have no idea why. You'

[00:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'felt this before, even if you never had a name for it. An app that\'s snappy at 9:00 in the morning and crawling by lunch. So, today, in this class, we\'re going to hunt down exactly what\'s hanging on to'

[00:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that box after you close it. We\'ll see how it tricks the browser\'s cleanup system into keeping it alive forever. And by the end, you\'ll watch that memory graph drop right back down to almost nothing. So, let\'s understand what\'s'

[01:05] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: The "one-button app" is shown alongside a "Heap - objects in memory" panel, illustrating boxes being added to memory.
AUDIO: 'really going on here. Let\'s start at the very beginning with one simple question. When you open that box, where does it actually go? Because when your code creates something, the browser has to store it somewhere in'

[01:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'memory. And that somewhere has a name. It\'s called the heap. Now, don\'t let the word scare you. The heap is just the big open space in memory where all your objects live. I want you to picture a warehouse with endless shelves. Every'

[01:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'object your app makes gets its own shelf. So, every time you open your box, the browser builds it and puts it on a shelf in that warehouse. Open it once, one shelf is used. And here\'s the part I'

[01:44] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: More boxes are added to the "Heap - objects in memory" panel.
AUDIO: 'really want you to hear. That, by itself, is completely normal. In a healthy app, you open the box, it takes a shelf, you close it, and that shelf gets cleared for the next thing.'

[01:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Objects are born, they do their job, and they get swept away. Our trouble today only starts when they refuse to leave that shelf. Now, here\'s the good news. Most of the time, you never have to think about any'

[02:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'of this at all. You open the box, you close it, and the shelf gets cleared automatically. You didn\'t write any code to clean it up. So, who did? JavaScript did. It comes with an'

[02:20] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: The "Heap - objects in memory" panel shows objects remaining, with a line indicating the garbage collector running.
AUDIO: 'automatic cleaner that has a name, the garbage collector. Let me say that one in plain English. The garbage collector is a background helper that hunts down the objects you no longer need and [music] clears them'

[02:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'out for you. Picture a janitor walking that warehouse every night. Any shelf that nobody needs anymore, they quietly empty it. You never call this janitor. You never schedule it. It just runs over and over,'

[02:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'keeping the whole place tidy. [music] So, now we\'ve got a real puzzle on our hands. If this janitor is cleaning up automatically, then how on earth does opening one little box a few hundred times leave you with a gigabyte of'

[02:58] [scene:diagram]
VISUAL: A diagram shows "window" connected to "app", illustrating the root of reachability.
AUDIO: 'memory? Hold on to that question, because the entire lesson turns on the answer. And that answer hides inside a single word. That word is reachable. Let\'s understand it slowly, because it'

[03:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'is the key to absolutely everything today. The garbage collector lives by exactly one rule. It only keeps objects that are still reachable. So, what does reachable actually mean?'

[03:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'It means the collector can still get to that object by following references, starting from a known starting point. And a reference is just one object holding on to another. Now, that starting point has a name, too. We call'

[03:36] [scene:diagram]
VISUAL: The diagram expands to show "window" connected to "app", which is connected to "{ Box }" and "{ data }", with "{ old Box }" shown as unreachable.
AUDIO: 'it the root. And in a browser, the root is basically the window, the top of everything. So, watch what the collector does. It starts at the window, and it walks every connection outward, from'

[03:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'object to object to object, like following a trail of strings. Every object it can reach along that trail, it marks as alive. And everything left over, everything with no trail leading'

[04:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to it, it sweeps away. And believe it or not, &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; that is the entire algorithm. It even has a name that tells you exactly what it does, mark and sweep. Mark everything you can reach, sweep away everything you'

[04:13] [scene:diagram]
VISUAL: A diagram shows "window" connected to "{ Box }" via a "forgotten reference".
AUDIO: 'can\'t. Okay, now here\'s where it gets interesting &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; because now the trap comes into focus. Look at that rule one more time. The collector will never ever delete'

[04:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'something it can still [music] reach. Even if you, the developer, are completely done with it. Even if you closed that box 10 minutes ago and will never look at it again. If just one tiny reference still points'

[04:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to it, the collector assumes you must still need it. So, it keeps it. And it gets worse. If that object points to more objects, every single one of them stays alive, too. One forgotten string'

[04:50] [scene:diagram]
VISUAL: The diagram shows "window" connected to "{ Box }", which is connected to "{ data }" and "{ list }", illustrating a leak.
AUDIO: 'can pin an entire chain of objects in memory. Picture one box tied by a single string to a hundred other boxes. The janitor sees that string leading somewhere, shrugs, [music]'

[05:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and leaves the whole pile sitting right where it is. So, let me tell you what a memory leak actually is because it is not what the name suggests. Nothing leaked out. Nothing escaped. A memory leak is simply memory the collector was never allowed'

[05:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to touch. [music] That\'s it. That\'s the whole villain of our story. So, let\'s stop talking theory and catch our first leak live with our one little box. Watch closely. [music]'

[05:28] [scene:code]
VISUAL: JavaScript code for `app.js` is shown, demonstrating `openBtn.onclick` and `closeBtn.onclick` functions, with `box.remove()` highlighted.
AUDIO: 'I\'ll walk you through it step by step. You click open and the box appears on the screen. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; Now, when it appeared, your code did something very reasonable. It saved that box in a variable so it could do things'

[05:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'with it later. Maybe update the text inside, &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; totally normal. Then you click close. On screen, poof, the box is gone. But here is what [music] you didn\'t do. You took the box off the page, but you never'

[05:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'cleared that variable. So the variable is still pointing right at it. And now that box is what we call a detached DOM node. Let me define that since it\'s the very first real leak of the day.'

[06:06] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: The Elements tab of DevTools shows HTML structure, and the "Heap - objects in memory" panel shows a "Detached div" still alive.
AUDIO: 'A detached DOM node is an element that has been pulled off the page, but is still being held by your JavaScript. And think about how strange that is. You can\'t see it. The collector can\'t remove it because your variable still reaches'

[06:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it. So it just floats there in the heap, fully built, useful to absolutely no one. Developers even have a nickname for these, zombie nodes, dead on the screen but still alive in memory.'

[06:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Now if you thought that one was sneaky, just wait because the nastiest leak of all usually isn\'t the box itself. It\'s the listener sitting on it. Let me define that first. An event listener is a function you'

[06:45] [scene:code]
VISUAL: JavaScript code shows `closeBtn.addEventListener` with a `save` function and `box.remove()`.
AUDIO: 'attach to an element so it can run when something happens, like a click. So on our box you probably attached a listener to that close button. Simple enough. But here comes the catch that gets'

[06:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'almost everybody. When you attach that function, the browser has to hold onto it. And a function quietly remembers every variable that was around it when it was born. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; So say your little function also touched some data, maybe the message text, maybe'

[07:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'a bigger object nearby. That listener is now keeping all of it alive. The box stays because the listener points to it. And the data stays because the listener remembers it.'

[07:22] [scene:diagram]
VISUAL: A diagram shows "window" connected to "onClose listener", which is connected to "{ Box + data }", labeled "a bridge you forgot to burn".
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; You closed the box on the screen, but you never removed its listener. So the browser just sits there holding the whole thing waiting for a click that is never ever coming. And the truly evil ones sit on the'

[07:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'window itself. Those outlive the box completely because nothing ever tears them down. It\'s a bridge you forgot to burn. All right, [music] let\'s zoom out now because one leaked box is not a'

[07:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'gigabyte. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; So, watch what happens when you actually use this app. You open the box, you close [music] it. Open, close. Open, close. Each time a brand new box gets built and'

[08:00] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: A memory graph shows a steadily increasing heap size, reaching 1 GB, labeled "memory never drops".
AUDIO: 'put on a shelf. And each time, thanks to that leftover variable and that leftover listener, the old one never gets cleared. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; So, they don\'t leave. They just stack up. Open and close that box 500 times'

[08:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'over a morning, which is nothing for a real app, and you are quietly carrying 500 dead boxes in memory all at once. Not a single one of them is on your'

[08:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'screen, but every single one of them is still holding its data. And that, right there, is exactly how one tiny button crept all the way up to a gigabyte. It was never one giant object. It was'

[08:36] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: Chrome DevTools Memory tab is shown, ready for a heap snapshot.
AUDIO: 'hundreds of tiny ones. Every one of them something the collector was forbidden to sweep. [music] A slow, silent pile up that you never actually see happening. So now, the big question.'

[08:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; How do we actually see these ghosts? Because you cannot fix what you cannot find. Let me show you the tool for the job. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; We open Chrome DevTools and we take something called a heap snapshot.'

[09:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; A heap snapshot is simply a photograph of every object in memory at one exact moment. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; So, here\'s the trick. We take one snapshot, we open and close our box a bunch of times, and then we'

[09:14] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: Chrome DevTools Memory tab shows "Retainers - path to the culprit".
AUDIO: '[music] take a second snapshot, and we compare the two. The objects that keep growing between those two photos, that is our leak, caught red-handed. And we can even filter straight down to detached nodes,'

[09:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and there they are. Row after row of dead boxes the user closed minutes ago. And then DevTools shows us the best part of the whole investigation. It\'s called the retaining path.'

[09:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'The retaining path is the exact chain of references keeping an object alive. Think of it as a map that leads you straight to the culprit. We just follow it upward, one link at a time, and it points right at that one'

[09:52] [scene:code]
VISUAL: JavaScript code for `app.js` shows a refactored `openBox` function with `onClose` and a return function.
AUDIO: 'forgotten variable or that one forgotten listener holding the whole pile hostage. No more guessing where your memory went. And now, at last, the fix. And honestly, it is simpler than the bug ever was.'

[10:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Here\'s rule number one, and if you remember nothing else from today, remember this one. Whatever you set up, you tear down. When the box closes, remove its listener and clear the variable that was holding'

[10:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it. Because the moment that last reference disappears, the box finally becomes unreachable. And the collector is finally free to sweep it away. Now, if you use a framework, this is'

[10:32] [scene:code]
VISUAL: JavaScript code shows `const cache = new WeakMap(); cache.set(boxEl, bigData);`.
AUDIO: 'exactly what a clean up function is for. React calls it on unmount. View calls it on unmounted. That function exists for this one single job. Remove whatever you added right before the thing leaves the'

[10:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'screen. And for the trickier cases, JavaScript hands us a special tool. It\'s called a weak map. Let me explain the name because the name is the whole idea. A weak map holds its reference so weakly'

[10:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that the garbage collector is allowed to just ignore it. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; A normal reference says, "Keep this alive no matter what." A weak reference says, "Keep it only while somebody else still needs it." So,'

[11:10] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: A memory graph shows a flat line at 48 MB after cleanup, labeled "memory stays flat all day".
AUDIO: 'the data quietly cleans itself up the very instant its real owner is gone. So, let\'s watch the payoff together because you have earned it. We add our cleanup.'

[11:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Now, when the box closes, its listener comes off and its variable gets cleared. [music] We reload the app and we start recording the memory again. Then we open and close that box 500 times, exactly like before.'

[11:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'But this time, keep your eyes on the graph. It stays flat. Every closed box gets swept the very second it leaves the screen. That gigabyte we started this class'

[11:47] [scene:text]
VISUAL: A summary of three points: "the collector only frees what it cannot reach", "a forgotten variable or listener keeps it reachable forever", and "clean up what you create -> your app stays light".
AUDIO: 'with, it now [music] holds perfectly steady at a few megabytes all day long. So let me give you the entire lesson back in one single breath. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; The collector only frees what it cannot'

[11:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'reach. A forgotten variable or a forgotten listener keeps [music] things reachable forever. So clean up what you create and your app stays light. That\'s it. [music] That is memory management.'

[12:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
